            <ArticleTitle>[Mesenteric cysts: rare cause of abdominal pain].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1072-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>For about two years a now 47-year-old woman had been suffering from heartburn, diarrhoea and constipation, at times also abdominal colic and a pressure sensation in the right lower abdomen. Physical examination and biochemical tests were according to age, except for an raised erythrocyte sedimentation rate of 25/50 mm. Ultrasound revealed an intraperitoneal echo-free multi-chambered structure, 11 x 5 x 2 cm, with dorsally increased echo density and smooth entry echo, apparently arising from the mesentery. Computed tomography confirmed these findings and at laparoscopy a cyst, 12 x 8 x 5 cm, was identified. It was filled with yellow clear fluid and arose from the mesentery of the ascending colon. The cyst was scooped out surgically. Histological examination showed a single-layered mesothelial coat. The postoperative course was unremarkable. At re-examination 8 months later the patient had remained symptom-free.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
